course: everyday
lesson: 469
title: The copier and the one who competes at everything
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Every time Lily gets something new, Grace turns up with it the next day: the sloth hairclip, the glitter pencil case, the same trainers. Lily is flattered, then annoyed. Then in literacy Grace reads out a story about a dinosaur who opens a bakery — Lily's idea, from Tuesday. Now Lily is really angry. She shows the teacher her planning book with Tuesday's date on it; the teacher has a quiet word, and Grace goes pink and changes her story. Copying clothes can be a compliment. Copying someone's work and calling it yours crosses a line.
explain: Kieran competes with Sam at everything: who's taller, who runs faster, who has more Robux, who can burp the alphabet furthest, who finished his chips first. «Beat ya, loser. Again.» Sam is worn out. One day he just says, «You win, mate. I'm not playing.» With no race to win, Kieran gets bored of it within a week. Lily's friend Jess compares everything too: «My house is bigger. My dog's cuter. We went to Spain for two weeks, you only went to Wales.» Lily stops arguing and says, «OK, cool.» With nothing to fight, Jess runs out of things to compare — and then asks about Wales, for real.
explain: Some copying steals credit. Whenever the teacher asks a question, Mia whispers her answer to herself and Zoe, next to her, shouts it first: «Photosynthesis!» Zoe gets the house point every time — until Mia starts writing her answers down and putting her hand straight up, and the teacher starts saying, «Zoe, your own answer, please.» Sam shows Kofi a sneaky chess trap; a week later Kofi tells the club, «I literally invented this, it's called the Kofi.» Sam laughs: «Bruh, I showed you that last Thursday.» Kofi goes red, and the club calls it «the Sam-Kofi» from then on.
explain: Rivalry can turn dangerous. Jake and Connor both want to be top scorer; in practice Jake starts going in late on Connor's ankles. «Soz, slipped.» The third time, Connor limps off. The coach benches Jake for a match: «You don't hurt your own team to win.» In Singapore, Mia's cousin Wei has a classmate who holds out his hand every morning: «Homework. Can or not, ah?» — Singapore English for «Can I?». Wei says no, politely. The boy fails the test, the teacher sees he's struggling and gives him extra help. Letting someone copy doesn't help them learn. And when Chloe's little cousin copies every photo pose she does, Mum says, «She looks up to you, love,» so they do a silly photo together for Nan's fridge. Kieran copies Jake's haircut, trainers and walk, until Jake calls him «mini-me» in front of everyone; Kieran gets his own haircut, with a shaved line, and loads of people like it. Ellie borrows Maddie's spelling tricks «for luck» and wins the class round; Maddie says «well done» through her teeth, makes new tricks, keeps them to herself, and wins next term.
fact: Copying someone's clothes or style is often a sign of admiration.
fact: Copying someone's work or ideas and saying they are yours is not fair; keeping dated notes of your ideas helps show they were yours.
fact: If someone keeps taking your answers or ideas, a teacher can help without a big drama.
fact: You don't have to join every contest; stepping out often makes a rivalry fizzle.
fact: Replying calmly, like «OK, cool», can end a contest with someone who compares everything.
fact: Competing is fine, but hurting a teammate to win is never fine.
fact: A light laugh can sometimes be enough to call out someone who takes credit for your idea.
fact: Letting a friend copy your homework stops them learning and can hide that they need help.
fact: Copying others just to fit in rarely works; being your own version usually works better.
fact: Losing with good grace and learning from it helps you get better, and rivals can push you to improve.
fact: A younger child who copies you often wants to be like you, and you can turn it into doing things together.
fact: In Singapore English «can or not?» means «can I?» or «is it OK?»; «soz» is slang for sorry.
exercise: Grace copied Lily's hairclip and trainers. What might that mean? | Grace admires her | Grace secretly hates her | Grace is a thief and a liar | Grace wants to be told off
exercise: Why was copying Lily's story worse than copying her clothes? | She claimed Lily's idea as her own work | Stories cost much more to make than clothes do | Dinosaurs can't open bakeries | It was a different day
exercise: How did Lily prove the story idea was hers? | Her planning book had Tuesday's date | She shouted in class | She asked her mum to phone Grace's mum about it | She tore up Grace's book
exercise: How did Sam stop Kieran's endless contests? | He said «You win, I'm not playing» | He beat Kieran at everything | He got a bigger lad to win for him | He moved to another school
exercise: Why did Kieran get bored of competing? | There was no race left to win | Sam started winning every race | He ran out of chips to bet | His mum told him off for it
exercise: Jess says «we went to Spain, you only went to Wales». How did Lily end the contest? | «OK, cool» | «Well, my house is bigger» | «Spain is boring anyway» | She cried and left
exercise: Zoe shouted out Mia's whispered answers. What helped? | Mia wrote answers down and put her hand up | Mia whispered wrong answers so Zoe would get them wrong | Mia shouted wrong answers | Mia stopped answering
exercise: Kofi called Sam's chess trap «the Kofi». How did Sam handle it? | He laughed and said he'd shown him last week | He quit the chess club and joined football instead | He punched Kofi | He said nothing ever
exercise: Why did the coach bench Jake? | He kept hurting Connor in practice to win | He scored too many goals and showed off about it | He was late for the match | He forgot his boots
exercise: Is competing to be top scorer wrong? | No, but hurting a teammate to win is | Yes, all competing is wrong in a team sport | Only if you win | Only for strikers
exercise: «Can or not, ah?» What is the classmate asking Wei? | Can I copy your homework? | Can you play later? | Can I have a sweet? | Can you lend me a pen?
exercise: Why was Wei right to say no? | Copying stops you learning | He wanted all the marks for himself | Homework is secret | The boy was rude
exercise: What happened after the boy failed the test? | The teacher saw he was struggling and helped | He got expelled | He copied someone else's answers instead next time | Wei got the blame
exercise: Chloe's little cousin copies every pose. What did Mum say? | She looks up to you, be flattered | She's making fun of you behind your back | Ignore her completely | Stop taking photos
exercise: Kieran copied Jake's haircut and walk, and got called «mini-me». What worked better for Kieran? | Getting his own style | Copying Jake even more | Shaving his head completely | Staying indoors all summer
exercise: Ellie used Maddie's memory tricks and won the spelling round. What was Maddie's best answer? | Say well done and make new tricks for next time | Tell everyone Ellie cheated and should give the prize back | Rip up Ellie's list | Quit spelling forever
exercise: «Soz, slipped,» says Jake. What does «soz» mean? | Sorry | So what | Shoes | Save
test: «grace copied my trainers, she's literally stealing my whole life». What's the fairer view? | Copying clothes is often admiration | Grace is a criminal and should be told off | Lily should change school | Trainers can't be copied
test: «just let him copy, it's only homework, what's the harm». What's the harm? | He doesn't learn and his struggling stays hidden | None at all, it's kind to help a mate who's stuck | It makes him smarter | Teachers like copying
test: Kieran: «bet I can eat my chips faster than you, bet ya, bet ya». What ends it fastest? | «You win, I'm not playing» | Eat yours twice as fast | Steal his chips | Tell him he's fat
test: Jake goes in late on your ankles «by accident» for the third time. What's the best move? | Tell the coach | Go in harder on his ankles | Quit football altogether | Pretend you didn't notice
test: Zoe keeps shouting your answers. You're fuming. What sorts it without drama? | Write answers down and let the teacher see | Whisper wrong answers to trick her so she looks silly | Shout «SHE'S A THIEF» in class | Stop learning anything
test: In a film, the rival gets his revenge by copying the hero's secret move. What happened in real life with the chess trap? | A laugh was enough and it got a fair name | Sam sued Kofi | They never spoke again | Kofi invented it really
test: Your little cousin copies everything you do and it's doing your head in. Kind idea? | Do something together, like a silly photo | Tell her she's annoying | Hide from her | Copy her back to wind her up
test: Jess: «my dog's cuter than yours». If Lily says «OK, cool» every time, what would most likely happen? | Jess runs out of things to compare | Jess gets more and more competitive | Lily loses her dog | Jess moves to Spain
test: Ellie borrowed Maddie's word list «for luck» and won. What's the wisest reaction? | Lose gracefully and make new tricks for next time | Accuse her in front of everyone of stealing the list | Never speak to Ellie again | Give up spelling
test: Kieran copies Jake's haircut to fit in. Most likely result? | He gets teased as a copy | Everyone thinks he's Jake | Jake thanks him | He becomes leader
test: Lily wrote her idea in her book with the date. Why does that help? | It shows when the idea was hers | Teachers love neat books | Dates make stories better | It stops Grace reading
exam: Identify the principal distinction between imitating a peer's clothing and presenting a peer's work as one's own. | The latter wrongly claims credit for another's effort | The former is unlawful whereas the latter is not | Neither is of any consequence | Both are invariably signs of admiration
exam: Given that a peer persistently challenges a pupil to contests of every kind, ascertain the conduct most likely to end the rivalry. | Declining calmly to participate further | Accepting every contest and striving to win | Enlisting older pupils to compete on one's behalf | Responding with insults of a personal nature
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the deliberate injury of a teammate in order to become the leading scorer? | It is impermissible however keen the competition | It is acceptable if described as accidental | It is a recognised tactic in junior football | It is permissible during practice sessions
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning permitting a classmate to copy one's homework. | It impedes his learning and conceals his need for assistance | It is the kindest form of assistance available to a struggling pupil | It improves his understanding of the subject | It has no consequences for either pupil
